#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.1"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "MUST"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Each measure and dimension is defined once, in version control, per domain.{1}*Example:* 'Gross enrolment ratio' is defined once; the dashboard, the published table and the international return all use that one definition."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "No measure name has two definitions."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.2"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Dashboards, natural-language queries and agents read measures through the semantic layer."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Dashboard and agent queries reference semantic-layer objects, not raw gold columns."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.3"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Each indicator carries metadata saying what it means and how to read it, how it is computed (numerator and denominator as separate measures, unit, reference-date rule), how it may be broken down, where its counts come from and how often, what coverage it needs before publication, and who owns which version of it.{1}*Example:* The metadata of the gross enrolment ratio names its numerator (enrolled learners) and denominator (official-age population), its unit, the census reference date, the breakdowns allowed and its owner."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "A check finds no indicator with a missing metadata field."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.4"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "SHOULD"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "An indicator with an international definition follows the UIS Official List of SDG 4 Indicators and the UIS education glossary, records the edition it follows, and documents any deviation in its metadata."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Indicators that map to international definitions name them; deviations are listed."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.5"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "A denominator from outside the platform, such as a population estimate by age, is versioned reference data that names its source and vintage.{1}*Example:* The population estimate used as the denominator is stored as reference data, 'population projections, 2024 vintage', so a figure computed with it can be reproduced after the next vintage arrives."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Indicators with external denominators reference a versioned reference table."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.6"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Measure and indicator definitions live in the same versioned project as the transformation models that produce their counts, are changed in the same review, and are validated in CI."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CI runs the semantic layer's validation on every change."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.7"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Published indicators use a portable subset of definitions (simple counts and sums, ratios, and derived expressions without time offsets), each indicator a ratio of additive counts on one gold table; any other construct has a recorded, tested mapping for each serving engine.{1}*Example:* A gross enrolment ratio is a count divided by a count on one gold table, which every engine computes alike. A twelve-month rolling attendance average is cumulative, so it needs a recorded mapping for each engine."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "A CI lint lists every definition outside the portable subset with its mapping."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.8"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Every artefact a consumer reads (engine-native metric objects, saved-query tables, dashboard datasets) is generated from the definitions by CI, never written by hand."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Generated artefacts carry a marker and are reproduced byte for byte by the generator."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.9"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "A parity test runs every saved query through every serving path and requires identical results.{1}*Example:* The saved query 'enrolment by district, 2026' returns the same total through the metric view and through the generated table; any difference fails CI."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "The parity report lists each saved query and serving path with a match."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "CAP-SEMANTICS.10"
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "Definitions can be exported in an open semantic interchange format once a released version is supported by the producing and consuming tools."
msgstr ""

#: docs/specifications/capabilities/cap-semantics.md
msgid "The export runs in CI and validates against the format's schema."
msgstr ""

